- What are the performance implications on CPUs lacking 128-bit intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit reasoning, non-branching logic, and careful boundary handling across hi/lo halves. The value is a power of two when exactly one bit in the 128-bit space is set.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value split into hi/lo; treat as two 64-bit lanes.
- Power-of-two check: a nonzero 64-bit word is a power of two if (x & (x-1)) == 0.
- Branchless composition: the single 1 must reside either in lo (and hi == 0) or in hi (and lo == 0).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch in C-like syntax (language-agnostic semantics)
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  unsigned int loCase = (lo != 0) && ((lo & (lo - 1)) == 0) && (hi == 0);
  unsigned int hiCase = (hi != 0) && ((hi & (hi - 1)) == 0) && (lo == 0);
  return (loCase || hiCase) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to an embedded environment with no 64-bit ops available?
- How would you verify correctness across all 128-bit values efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of non-trivial bit rearrangements across 128 bits without loops. The task blends endian concepts with mask-shift patterns, common in low-level data handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit data as hi/lo halves
- Byte-wise reversal using masks and shifts
- Endianness-aware data transformations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline (actual C-like code uses uint128 struct)
static uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t t = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  t = ((t & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((t & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  return (t << 32) | (t >> 32);
}
uint128 endianSwap128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = bswap64(x.lo); r.lo = bswap64(x.hi); return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable endianness at compile time? 
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Reversing 128 bits tests a candidate's ability to decompose 128-bit data into two 64-bit lanes and apply a branch-free bit-reversal at the lower level.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value represented as {hi, lo}
- constant-time 64-bit bit-reversal using masks
- composing to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(v){
  v = ((v >>> 1) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((v & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  v = ((v >>> 2) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((v & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  v = ((v >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((v & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  v = ((v >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  v = ((v >>> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  v = (v >>> 32) | (v << 32);
  return v;
}
function reverseBits128(x){
  // x.hi, x.lo are 64-bit values represented as BigInt
  return { hi: reverse64(x.lo), lo: reverse64(x.hi) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type if provided?
- What are the trade-offs of using 64-bit reversals vs a single 128-bit table lookup on platforms with broad SIMD support?

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests branchless bit-twiddling and cross-half reasoning on a 128-bit type. Parity is a classic low-level primitive with real-world use in checksums and error-detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless parity computation
- XOR folding techniques across 64-bit halves
- 128-bit struct handling without loops

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo ^= lo >> 32;
  lo ^= lo >> 16;
  lo ^= lo >> 8;
  lo ^= lo >> 4;
  lo ^= lo >> 2;
  lo ^= lo >> 1;
  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi ^= hi >> 32;
  hi ^= hi >> 16;
  hi ^= hi >> 8;
  hi ^= hi >> 4;
  hi ^= hi >> 2;
  hi ^= hi >> 1;
  return (lo & 1) ^ (hi & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to count parity modulo any odd number (e.g., 3) with similar tricks?
- Could you prove the branchless reduction yields correct parity for all 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

